---
import "../sternzeit/sternzeit.css";
import "./nachtgestirn.css";

/** Lambert's law, Oren-Nayar's and Hapke's of 1963 and 2012 from the side: where each sends the light falling on a patch. */
interface Props {
    id?: string;
}
const { id = "lobes" } = Astro.props;
const LAWS = [
    ["lambert", "Lambert", "a matte ball"],
    ["orenNayar", "Oren-Nayar", "a rough ball"],
    ["hapke", "Hapke 1963", "a powder"],
    ["hapke2012", "Hapke 2012", "a rough powder"],
] as const;
---

<figure class="lobes-figure" id={id} data-lobes>
    <div class="lobes-row" data-export="reflectance" data-export-timeless>
        {LAWS.map(([law, name, what]) => (
            <div class="lobes-panel">
                <svg data-law={law} role="img" aria-label={`The light a patch of surface sends to each side under ${name}'s law`}></svg>
                <p class="lobes-label"><span class={`tiles-name law-${law}`}>{name}</span> <span class="tiles-value">{what}</span></p>
            </div>
        ))}
    </div>
    <p class="view-status"><span data-field="lobes"></span></p>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        A patch of the Moon's surface from the side under four reflectance laws: Lambert's, Oren and Nayar's with a
        roughness of 0.5 rad, Hapke's lunar law of 1963 as Jensen and colleagues use it, and his model of 2012 with Sato
        and colleagues' parameters for the Moon. The patch's normal points up, the arrow points to the Sun, and the shape around the
        patch is how much light it sends to each direction in that plane, all four on one scale;
        the dashed half circle is a Lambert surface lit from straight above. Lambert's sends the most straight up
        whatever the Sun does, Oren-Nayar's leans back towards the Sun, and both of Hapke's throw the most straight back
        at it.
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import { lobesFigure } from "./lobes";

    for (const root of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("figure[data-lobes]")) lobesFigure(root);
</script>
